```kotlin
// Leaks: inner class implicitly holds a reference to MainActivity
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
    private val listener = object : SomeLongLivedService.Listener {
        override fun onEvent() { updateUi() }   // implicitly calls MainActivity.this.updateUi()
    }
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        SomeLongLivedService.register(listener)   // service outlives the Activity
    }
}

// Fixed: explicit lifecycle-aware unregistration
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
    private val listener = object : SomeLongLivedService.Listener {
        override fun onEvent() { updateUi() }
    }
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        SomeLongLivedService.register(listener)
    }
    override fun onDestroy() {
        SomeLongLivedService.unregister(listener)   // breaks the reference before the leak matters
        super.onDestroy()
    }
}
```

The fix here isn't a `WeakReference` at all — it's simpler and more reliable: explicitly unregistering in `onDestroy` breaks the reference at the moment it stops being needed, rather than relying on the garbage collector to eventually notice a weak reference is only weakly held. A `WeakReference` is the right tool when you genuinely can't guarantee an unregister call will happen; an explicit unregister is preferable whenever you can guarantee it, because it's deterministic rather than dependent on GC timing.
